{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794232794,"nonce":"GnsxIr3IHznqArAx4tFZuRMdyAk=","title":"Found on the demo box (Oracle, 2 cores) on 2026-11-09. Namespace `scarab-steps` holds 472 step Pods:","message":"- 459 Completed, the oldest 72 days old;\n- 1 Error and 3 Init:Error;\n- 9 Pending (`scarab-image-a{1,2,3}-*`).\n\nThe Pending Pods belong to Run 01a04915-31b0-7b91-aede-5a842d3244cb, which dead-lettered on 2026-08-28. They have failed scheduling ~21,300 times (\"Insufficient cpu\") over 72 days.\n\nThe executor's comments speak of \"a kept Pod\" (crates/scarab-executor-k8s/src/lib.rs ~3087), and the step Secrets are deleted when the Pod finishes. I found no garbage collection or retention policy for the finished Pods themselves, in the code or the docs.\n- On a small node they pile up in etcd and in `kubectl get pods`.\n- The Pending ones keep the scheduler busy.\n\n**To do:** decide and implement a retention for finished step Pods, for example delete once the Attempt is recorded and logs are captured, or a TTL. Also delete the Pods of a Run that is terminal (dead-lettered, cancelled). #121 tears down a poison launch going forward, but nothing collects older leftovers. The demo's backlog can be cleared once by hand:\n\n    sudo k3s kubectl -n scarab-steps delete pods --field-selector=status.phase=Succeeded\n    sudo k3s kubectl -n scarab-steps delete pods -l scarab.io/run=01a04915-31b0-7b91-aede-5a842d3244cb\n\nNot done yet; left for the owner.","files":null}]}